Gastroesophageal reflux disease
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Retrosternal heartburn, Acid regurgitation, Exacerbation when supine, Relief with antacids

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-027
Reason for Visit: Severe, burning chest pain radiating to the throat, waking the patient from sleep and causing choking and panic.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, acute-onset retrosternal chest pain waking a patient from sleep. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was strictly mandated to rapidly evaluate for acute coronary syndrome, perform a clinical assessment, and definitively distinguish between ischemic cardiac pain and severe esophageal spasm/reflux prior to the availability of routine general practice services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up gasping for air with a terrible burning pressure in my chest, and I can taste battery acid in the back of my throat."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports severe retrosternal burning chest pain that began 3 hours after falling asleep. The episode immediately followed a late, heavy, spicy meal. Reports regurgitation of sour and highly acidic gastric contents into the mouth upon waking, accompanied by a sudden appearance of slightly salty fluid in the mouth (water brash). Notes a chronic non-productive cough, particularly worse at night, and persistent throat clearing in the early morning. Symptoms are severely exacerbated when lying flat or supine. Reports partial, transient relief of symptoms following administration of oral liquid antacids.
Functional Status: Pacing the room anxiously, refusing to lie down due to fear of worsening the chest pain or choking.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding cardiovascular risk; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious and uncomfortable, frequently burping and clearing the throat. No diaphoresis, pallor, or shortness of breath at rest.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals regular heart sounds with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. No reproducible chest wall tenderness. Chest is clear to auscultation bilaterally, ruling out gross aspiration. Abdomen is soft with mild epigastric tenderness on deep palpation, but absolute absence of guarding or rebound. The presentation clearly matches the diagnostic tetrad: retrosternal heartburn, acid regurgitation, exacerbation when supine, and relief with antacids.
Environmental Observations: Empty antacid bottles and remnants of a heavy, spicy meal on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Consumed over-the-counter liquid antacid tonight with minimal lasting effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GORD) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe nocturnal episode)
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome (Myocardial Infarction) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of diaphoresis, lack of radiation to the arm/jaw, and the specific triggering of symptoms by a large meal, recumbency, and acidic regurgitation into the oropharynx.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain being primarily retrosternal and characterized by acidic regurgitation into the throat, rather than isolated, gnawing epigastric pain relieved by food.

Achalasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute burning nature of the pain and acid regurgitation, rather than progressive, painless dysphagia to both solids and liquids with regurgitation of bland, undigested food.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe retrosternal chest pain waking them from sleep, a classic high-risk scenario necessitating immediate medical evaluation to exclude atypical acute coronary syndrome. An urgent after-hours home visit safely and definitively ruled out acute cardiac ischemia based on the clinical presentation and examination. Clinical assessment confirmed the pain was driven by severe nocturnal acid reflux secondary to a late, heavy meal and loss of gravitational clearance while supine. Diagnosing this non-cardiac etiology at the bedside prevented an unnecessary, highly stressful emergency ambulance dispatch and hospital admission, safely resolving the patient's acute anxiety with targeted high-dose acid suppression.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Pantoprazole 40mg daily for 4 weeks. Administered an oral viscous lidocaine/antacid suspension on-site with rapid symptomatic improvement.
Positive Behavioral Change: Elevate the head of your bed using 15cm solid blocks under the top bed legs. By mechanically raising your upper body, you use gravity to physically trap the stomach acid below the esophageal sphincter while you sleep, directly preventing the acid from washing up into your chest and causing these severe nighttime attacks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bed risers or a specialized wedge pillow.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to assess response to the proton pump inhibitor and consider endoscopy if symptoms persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the chest pain returns and feels crushing or heavy, radiates to your left arm or jaw, or is accompanied by a cold sweat and shortness of breath, call 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While the clinical assessment points to severe reflux, any new or different type of severe chest pain must always be evaluated as a potential cardiac event.
Clinical Course: The daily medication will stop your stomach from producing acid, allowing the chemical burns in your esophagus to heal over the next few weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The pain is originating from acid burning your food pipe. However, if you develop difficulty swallowing solid food, or if you ever vomit material that looks like coffee grounds, you must see a doctor urgently.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The valve separating your stomach and your chest relaxed after your large, late meal. When you lay flat, the highly acidic stomach contents flowed backward, causing severe chemical burns to the sensitive lining of your chest tube. The prescribed medication completely blocks the acid pumps in your stomach, giving the tissue time to heal.
Actionable Steps: Do not eat any food or drink alcohol within three hours of going to bed. Eating late fills the stomach and increases the pressure against the valve; going to sleep with an empty stomach ensures there is no volume of food or acid physically available to reflux into your chest.

